ef 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531523 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531523 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531523 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531523 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d29ba8d5-82d0-52b6-939b-119fe78d8db4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531541 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531541 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531541 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186530871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531541 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eaab585b-caae-5298-a3ee-f10042b952cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531579 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531579 #edb/ref 1069 "q-30282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531579 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531579 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9a/9a448574651a74ef7180549827e05ee2d790cd9fa89c6c85adf08d8444ceaadf.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({10}^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531579 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33e5e3ff-cf8e-5556-9914-9fd8f6506ea2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531579 #edb/ref 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({10}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3,$ so $c=-3.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({10}^{x})-3.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({10}^{x})-3 \\\\ 1 & =k({10}^{0})-3 \\\\ 1 & =k(1)-3 \\\\ 1 & =k-3 \\\\ 4 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=4({10}^{x})-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531604 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531604 #edb/ref 1069 "q-26914" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531604 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531604 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4({2}^{x})+4?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531604 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6bfc4a3c-5e7b-532b-95bd-4e49a1efa533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531604 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we plot the graph of $y={2}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/6d/6d5b359544227b89d1dc5ca9967c9dfcda84bf5345184063a1d0deef994c3692.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=4({2}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,4),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/86/86b10c71101ae46d4c99335874eb3cd75abc416c0d9a3b4552f6f6a52644aa2f.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=4({2}^{x})+4.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,4)$ to $(0,8),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/05/052daab57bdb99d53853e8d7d2cc7a059f9f24f715b1340857407d5a44c68acd.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531613 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531613 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18519" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531613 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531613 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/95/9507e060ddd7de93489b236b48856870e2c884eba69f63ac67662dfd1c061c17.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$  Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531613 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8412e9ee-2ea9-5393-944f-a1233b5c49f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531613 #edb/ref 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1,$ so $c=1.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})+1.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,3.5),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=3.5$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})+1 \\\\ 3.5 & =k({2}^{0})+1 \\\\ 3.5 & =k(1)+1 \\\\ 3.5 & =k+1 \\\\ 2.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=2.5({2}^{x})+1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531635 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531635 #edb/ref 1069 "q-26915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531635 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531635 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=6({5}^{x})+4?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531635 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "be426e06-620a-5f1c-9841-393e8911826d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531635 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we plot the graph of $y={5}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/84/8490920052778795ca03072fd0a6933b4abc5756f2e75ae9f61863d3726dd5c8.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=6({5}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $6.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,6),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/b5/b5deca2c07bc8c8a5e58c28a6ca2449268999a54b21407380ae168aafabd7a5d.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=6({5}^{x})+4.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,6)$ to $(0,10),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/57/57ac1f2daa7ee44b5cf9fbd2d4c9ce8a25edc3a3053af4d9164b7f8e83b9e00f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
